LeanDA security in Lean 4

The library PqDas (PqDas.lean and PqDas/) is Benedikt Wagner's formalization of erasure code commitments, ported from https://github.com/b-wagn/fv-pq-da at commit 4475179c19296acf3469d7d552a3186ad1feda74, and is licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0, a copy of which is in LICENSE.

Changes from that commit: every file of PqDas was modified to build on VCVio afa6593f15e1e79ecb3cf22fc56191cecb452bb0 and Lean v4.34.1 (measure-based probability notation and lemmas, experiments observed as measures, a few lemmas restated for equality in distribution, comments), and PqDas/AxiomCheck.lean pins the axioms with #guard_msgs; each modified file says so under its module docstring. The library LeanDa and the root module LeanDa.lean are new.

The definitions of PqDas that LeanDA's end statements need (the erasure code and the consistency of opened cells, namespace PqDas) are in ../Statement/LeanDa.lean, unchanged and under the same license; beside them, the same file holds work derived from his notions, under the same license: arguments of knowledge with oracle access (LeanDa.OracleArgument, its perfect completeness, StraightLineExtractor) and his completeness, position-binding, code-binding and knowledge-soundness games moved to the random-permutation model (completenessExperiment, positionBindingGame, codeBindingGame, knowledgeSoundnessGame); the argument interface Nark with its perfect completeness and extractors is in PqDas/Defs.lean, unchanged and moved back from ../Statement/LeanDa.lean when the end statements took arguments that query the random-permutation model's oracles (LeanDa.OracleArgument, his arguments of knowledge with oracle access); the rest of his definitions (the vector commitment and code checker interfaces, Wagner's knowledge-soundness experiment, the relation of the scheme's argument, the code-binding adversary, his reductions and their random-oracle simulation) are in PqDas/Defs.lean, unchanged and moved from ../Statement/LeanDa.lean when the end statements stopped naming them; PqDas keeps the erasure code commitment, the random-oracle runtime and the scheme (PqDas/Target/, PqDas/RomRuntime.lean) and the proofs. LeanDA's code-binding end statement assumes the argument's knowledge soundness in the random-permutation model, Wagner's knowledge-soundness notion with the hash over the permutation, the argument's prover and verifier querying the model's oracles and its extractor reading the adversary's transcript; its proof (LeanDa/StraightTree.lean, LeanDa/StraightBad.lean, LeanDa/Straight.lean, LeanDa/StraightBound.lean) is a straight-line argument built on Wagner's framework, his code-binding game, his knowledge-soundness reduction and his membership test, in place of his forking lemma; his theorems and reductions are kept unchanged, and his forking route, with his terms bounded (LeanDa/KsRpm.lean, LeanDa/WpbBound.lean, LeanDa/ForkBound.lean), proves, for an oracle-free argument, the weaker bound the end statement had before (leanDa_codeBinding_fork); the end theorems are in LeanDa/PiOnly.lean. PqDas/Target/Scheme.lean states the core of the completeness proof on its own (honest_verify).
